The '''History of Guajicutea (2012–2020)''' covers the period of Partido Bukaí rule under [[Mariana Torres]] and [[Rafael Meraí]], when Guajicutea shifted from restoration politics to a more stable growth cycle tied to tourism, services, and regional middle-class demand. The economy expanded as the neighboring societies of [[Lacashe]], [[Asikyira]], and [[Balboa]] developed larger middle classes that travelled more frequently. By the end of the decade, the country had also moved into a new social settlement marked by stronger labour politics, immigration reform, and a slower demographic trajectory. The era ended with the approach of the covid pandemic in 2020.

== Political background ==

[[Mariana Torres]] and the [[Partido Bukaí]] inherited a country that was more stable than it had been in the 2000s but still shaped by the legacy of the Mera years. Torres held the presidency until 2016, when [[Rafael Meraí]] succeeded her and carried the same governing party through the rest of the decade. The state remained suspicious of concentrated power, yet it also had more room to pursue long-term planning. Unlike the emergency politics of the previous decade, this period was defined less by crisis management and more by expansion, normalization, and gradual reform.

Torres remained broadly popular, and Meraí inherited that popularity after 2016. Their governments benefited from a calmer public mood, a steadier economy, and the fact that many voters were now more concerned with wages, housing, and mobility than with the old succession crisis. The party did not become a single-party machine in the same way the PUP had, but it remained the dominant force of the decade.

== Economy and employment ==

Growth in the 2010s was driven by tourism, transport, construction, and an expanding services economy. The broader regional economy helped: as the middle classes of [[Lacashe]], [[Asikyira]], and [[Balboa]] grew, more people travelled for leisure, shopping, and short holidays. Guajicutea benefited from proximity, coastline, and established resort infrastructure. New hotels, apartment blocks, restaurants, marinas, and transit corridors followed.

The government also managed to pay off the [[Adonian Community]] loan earlier than expected. That gave Torres more room to spend on infrastructure and social reform, and it reduced the sense that the state was living permanently on borrowed money. At the same time, the economy became more diversified. Tourism remained central, but it was joined by call centers, customer support, administrative outsourcing, logistics, and finance-adjacent services for Sartan firms. These sectors were attractive because Guajicutea had a large bilingual urban workforce and lower labour costs than its neighbors.

Employment shifted away from the crisis-era pattern of reliance on a few high-risk sectors. Construction remained strong in resort zones, while service-sector jobs spread into the capital and the northern coast. The result was steady growth without the explosive expansion of the 1950s and 1960s.

