== Labour politics returns ==

The decade also saw a return of stronger union politics. By the mid-2010s, workers in hospitality, logistics, and public-facing services had begun organizing again as wages lagged behind growth in resort profits and urban rents. Strikes in 2016 and 2018 signaled that the calm of the early restoration era had given way to a more active labour landscape.

The 2018 strikes were especially important. They helped push the government toward the '''National Minimum Wage Law''', which established a wage floor that rose each year with inflation. The law was intended to stop employers from holding down pay while consumer prices rose, especially in tourism and service sectors that depended on large numbers of lower-paid workers.

Unions did not regain the confrontational power they had in earlier periods, but they became a permanent feature of politics again. The state had to negotiate rather than simply manage labour from above.

== Social reforms ==

In 2018, Guajicutea also recognised same-sex marriage. The reform fit the broader social atmosphere of the decade: urban, tourism-oriented, and more open to external cultural currents than in the past. It also reflected the influence of younger urban voters and the gradual normalization of rights-based politics after the repression of the Mera years.

The same year, the government ended national military service. The decision reduced a long-standing civic obligation that had become less useful in a period when the state no longer expected the same kind of mass mobilization. It also appealed to younger voters, who increasingly saw compulsory service as expensive, outdated, and poorly matched to a service-based economy.

== Immigration and population ==

Population growth slowed sharply in this period. Birthrates, already falling during the 2000s, moved below replacement by the later 2010s. The baby-boom generation was aging into middle and later adulthood, and fewer children were being born. As a result, growth increasingly depended on immigration rather than natural increase.

The government reworked immigration policy in 2016. It introduced golden visas for foreign investors and made it easier for foreign workers to enter the country, though inflows remained modest. In 2017, it added a digital nomad visa, which became especially popular with remote workers and long-stay visitors in Waribá, Waku Bá, and the resort districts. The reforms were meant to support construction, hospitality, care work, and remote-service industries without forcing the state to rely entirely on local labour shortages. They also kept the economy flexible as the native-born working-age population stopped expanding as quickly.

