== The 2019 hurricane ==

In 2019, a major hurricane struck Guajicutea and damaged many port facilities, especially in [[Waribá]]. The storm hit tourism hard, because port access, cruise turnover, and waterfront services all slowed at once. Hotels and transport operators lost revenue, and some construction and repair work was redirected to recovery.

The hurricane did not produce the same kind of island-wide collapse that earlier disasters had caused. The wider economy was more diversified than it had been in the 1970s, and the service sector could absorb some of the shock. Even so, the tourism industry in Waribá suffered a clear downturn, and the storm reminded the country that its growth model still depended on exposed coastal infrastructure.

== Public mood and culture ==

The culture of the 2010s was more self-confident than that of the 2000s. Tourism-generated consumer culture continued to spread through urban life, but there was also a stronger sense that Guajicutea had become a normal middle-income country rather than a post-crisis society. Restaurants, music venues, beach districts, shopping corridors, and short-term rental housing became more visible in city life.

At the same time, the decade was marked by renewed attention to labour and social rights. The strikes of 2016 and 2018, the minimum wage law, same-sex marriage, and the end of military service all signaled a broader rebalancing between market growth and public regulation. The government tried to keep the tourism boom politically acceptable by pairing development with social reforms.

Publicly, the period felt more stable than the 2000s. Privately, many families worried about housing costs, wages, and the lack of children. The end of population growth made the question of future labour supply more visible, while the 2019 hurricane suggested that coastal prosperity still depended on fragile infrastructure.

